  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 250 PM CDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nEarly afternoon conditions across the forecast area range from \nmostly sunny skies and brisk northerly winds across northeast SD and \nwest central MN while central SD is experiencing more clouds and \nsome pesky sprinkles and brief showers. Overall, temperatures have \nclimbed into the low to mid 60s this afternoon.\n\nThe center of a sfc high pressure system will build into the area \ntonight through Friday morning setting up a bit of a tricky \ntemperatures forecast. Clouds are likely to hang tough across \ncentral SD tonight while northeast SD and west central MN remain \nclear to partly cloudy. With a dry air mass in place with dew point \ntemperatures in the low to mid 30s and a combination of light winds \nand those clear skies, the setup could be right for some of the \ncoolest temperatures of the season so far. Most of the forecast \narea, outside of northeast SD and west central MN will see overnight \nlows in the upper 30s to mid 40s. However, the northeast corner of \nSD and west central MN could dip into the mid to upper 30s prior to \ndaybreak Friday. One of the key factors in this will be sky \ncondition. Most of the overnight should remain clear but guidance \ndoes hint at high clouds encroaching into the James Valley and some \nlocales farther east by about 09Z-12Z. If this were to occur, \nreadings in that mid 30s range might be hard to achieve. Did lower \nvalues in these zones closer to NBM's 25th percentile. HREF/REFS \nguidance probabilities of seeing temperatures cooler than 37 degrees \nrange from about 10-25 percent for the northeast corner of SD and \nwest central MN. Did insert areas of frost wording into the forecast \nfor those zones overnight and will highlight the threat in an SPS \nbut for now will hold just shy of issuing any frost headline.\n\nTemperatures are expected to remain below normal through Friday as \nhigh pressure maintains control and shifts east of the region by the \nafternoon. Southeast breezes will return to central SD in the \nafternoon with gusts up to 25 mph possible. The next system \napproaches the area Friday night into Saturday. Low pressure and a \ncold front will pass through the region at that time and deliver a \n20-30 percent chance for showers mainly across the northern and \nnortheastern half of the forecast area on Saturday. Prior to the \nfropa on Saturday, there will be enough of a window to see temps \nwarm into the upper 60s to low 70s, but it will all be tied to the \ntiming of that front. Conditions dry out for the latter half of the \nweekend as temps cool back below normal. This will spill over into \nMonday before the next surge of warmer than normal air returns \nTuesday into the middle of next week.",
